    "provinces": {
      "_comment": "Categorical, after the USGS map 'Geologic provinces of the world': a view of the current state, derived when a frame is drawn (core provinces.rs; the world is not changed). First rule that applies wins. Large igneous province: a mafic volcanic pile of at least a fifth of the recorded LIP thickness above the crust's own basalt. Oceanic crust by age (0-20, 20-65, over 65 Ma); a thickened island arc is an orogen. Orogen: active collision or arc, a collision belt from the last 500 Myr, or an Andean range over 1.5 km lifted in the last 50 Myr. Extended crust: rift, passive margin, or crust thinned below 20 km. Basin: over 3 km of sediment and sedimentary rock (or subsiding under over 1 km). Shield: old continental crust with basement at the surface (under 0.2 km of cover, or crystalline surface rock). Platform: the rest, under a thinner sedimentary cover. Continental classes are generalised to their neighbours' majority.",
